course: dictionary
lesson: 10489
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and you cannot tell which one is meant until you read the context. Turn is a good example: it can mean to move around a centre, to change direction, to take your chance in a game, or to become something else.
explain: «A boy crossed a street and never saw the car turning towards him.» Here turning means changing direction, so the car was swinging round to face him. Try the swap: if you put "moving around a centre" in its place, the sentence makes no sense. The context chose the meaning.
explain: Turn also appears in common phrases such as take turns, turn on the light, turn up (arrive) and turn down (say no). «Heat exhaustion can turn into heatstroke» means it changes into something else. Look at the clues around the word first, then test your meaning in the sentence.
fact: Turn can be a verb (to turn, turns, turned, turning) or a noun (a turn).
fact: To turn means to move around a centre, as a wheel turns.
fact: A turn can be your chance to do something in a game or in order: it's your turn.
fact: «Turn» can mean become, as in «the leaves turn red» or «she turned 9», which means she became 9 years old.
fact: A quarter turn is 90 degrees, a half turn is 180 degrees, and a full turn is 360 degrees.
exercise: «He never saw the car turning towards him.» In this sentence, turning means | Becoming something new | Taking a turn in a game | *Changing direction | Moving around a centre like a wheel
exercise: «Sam, it's your turn to roll the dice.» What does turn mean here? | To change direction | To move around a centre | To become | *Your chance to do something in order
exercise: How many degrees is a half turn? | *180 | 90 | 270 | 360
test: «She turned 9 last week.» What does turned mean here? | To move around a centre | *To become, so she became 9 years old | To change direction | To take a turn
test: «Take turns with your sister when you play.» What does take turns mean? | *Do things one after another, each in order | Become something else | Move around a centre | Change direction
exam: Which phrase means to switch the light off? | *Turn off the light | Turn on the light | Turn up the light | Turn a page of the light
